    "result_text": "I added `login_required` and applied it everywhere `authed_only` was used. I couldn't import the app or run any tests, because Bash needed approval each time I tried. I did check with search that no `authed_only` references are left and that each file's `login_required` import and usage counts match.\n\n**What I found:** `authed_only` was imported and used as a decorator in 12 modules but was never defined. Importing the app would have failed. `login_required` fills that gap.\n\n**The decorator** is in `CTFd/utils/decorators/__init__.py`. It works like this:\n- **Logged-in users:** they go straight through to the view.\n- **Browser requests:** unauthenticated users are redirected to `auth.login`, with `next=` set so they return to the page afterward.\n- **JSON and event-stream requests:** these get a 403 instead of a redirect, so API clients and the notification stream don't receive an HTML login page. `registered_only` already handles these requests the same way.\n\n**Where it's applied** (all former `authed_only` uses, 37 decorator sites):\n- **Pages:**\n  - `/settings`\n  - `/profile` and `/user`\n  - `/team`, `/teams/join`, `/teams/new`\n- **Events:** the notification stream.\n- **API:**\n  - `shares`, `unlocks` and `solutions`\n  - `tokens`\n  - the `/me` endpoints on `users` and `teams`\n  - the challenge attempt and similar endpoints\n\nI also moved `login_required` to the right spot in the three multi-line imports so isort should stay happy.\n\nI left `registered_only` alone. It sends visitors to the register page rather than login, and `/teams/invite` seems to rely on that.\n\nPlease run the test suite and `isort --check` before merging."
  },
